WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.540
We want to turn h of Z and P into
polynomials in P while preserving

00:00:04.540 --> 00:00:09.300
its norm identity. Replace
only Z, and keep P unchanged.

00:00:10.100 --> 00:00:14.840
The substitutions one, negative one,
and i are fourth roots of unity:

00:00:15.600 --> 00:00:19.030
each satisfies Z to the fourth equals one.

00:00:19.380 --> 00:00:23.260
They therefore respect the four
cycle relation and define ring maps.

00:00:24.180 --> 00:00:29.013
Recall the decomposition into
H zero, plus Z times H one,

00:00:29.120 --> 00:00:34.487
plus Z squared times H two,
plus Z cubed times H three.

00:00:34.820 --> 00:00:37.620
Every H is still a polynomial in P.

00:00:38.220 --> 00:00:44.104
At Z equal to one, the four
weights are one, one, one, one.

00:00:44.790 --> 00:00:48.460
We get the sum of all four H polynomials.

00:00:49.280 --> 00:00:52.860
At Z equal to negative one,
the weights become one,

00:00:53.260 --> 00:00:55.570
negative one, one, negative one.

00:00:55.920 --> 00:01:00.560
Thus h of negative one and P is H zero

00:01:00.700 --> 00:01:05.400
minus H one plus H two minus H three.

00:01:05.760 --> 00:01:09.700
Both of these first two evaluated
polynomials have integer coefficients.

00:01:10.680 --> 00:01:14.520
At Z equal to i, the weights are one, i,

00:01:15.200 --> 00:01:19.687
negative one, negative i. Collect
the real terms as H zero minus H two,

00:01:19.780 --> 00:01:25.320
and the imaginary terms as i times
the difference H one minus H three.

00:01:25.580 --> 00:01:29.020
This evaluated polynomial has
Gaussian integer coefficients.

00:01:30.520 --> 00:01:32.560
We could also substitute negative i.

00:01:32.880 --> 00:01:37.280
This conjugates the coefficients of h
of i and P, while leaving P unchanged.

00:01:38.380 --> 00:01:40.360
Its polynomial is already determined.

00:01:40.960 --> 00:01:45.380
Coefficient conjugation here is different
from star: for Gaussian coefficients,

00:01:45.940 --> 00:01:49.860
star conjugates the coefficients
and also reverses the powers of P.

00:01:51.060 --> 00:01:53.800
Let epsilon be one of
our three chosen roots.

00:01:54.340 --> 00:01:58.140
Follow one term with real
coefficient h sub a b:

00:01:58.460 --> 00:02:02.500
h sub a b times Z to
the a times P to the b.

00:02:03.100 --> 00:02:05.440
First evaluate Z at epsilon.

00:02:05.440 --> 00:02:11.040
The term becomes h sub a b times
epsilon to the a times P to the b.

00:02:11.560 --> 00:02:16.100
Now take star: conjugate epsilon
and reverse the power of P.

00:02:16.920 --> 00:02:20.060
We obtain h sub a b times conjugate

00:02:20.060 --> 00:02:22.980
epsilon to the a times
P to the negative b.

00:02:23.660 --> 00:02:28.220
Follow the other path. First take star
in the original two variable ring:

00:02:28.640 --> 00:02:33.600
the term becomes h sub a b times Z to
the negative a times P to the negative b.

00:02:33.600 --> 00:02:36.020
Then evaluate Z at epsilon.

00:02:36.480 --> 00:02:40.080
The result is h sub a b times epsilon to

00:02:40.080 --> 00:02:43.920
the negative a times P to the
negative b. The endpoints agree,

00:02:43.920 --> 00:02:48.620
because conjugate epsilon equals
epsilon inverse on the unit circle.

00:02:49.100 --> 00:02:53.180
Applying this term by term proves
that evaluation commutes with star.

00:02:54.380 --> 00:02:57.240
Evaluation preserves
multiplication and star.

00:02:57.760 --> 00:03:02.160
Therefore h of Z and P times its
star equals four u squared becomes

00:03:02.160 --> 00:03:06.820
h of epsilon and P times its
own star equals four u squared.

00:03:07.460 --> 00:03:10.960
The relation P to the u squared
equals one is still in force.

00:03:11.800 --> 00:03:15.040
All three evaluated polynomials
retain the same norm.

00:03:16.100 --> 00:03:19.513
For u equal to three, that
norm is still thirty six.

00:03:19.800 --> 00:03:23.340
Once the parity check permits division
by two in the coefficient ring,

00:03:23.650 --> 00:03:27.350
halving both factors will divide
the norm by four, giving nine.

00:03:27.740 --> 00:03:29.980
That normalization is the next step.

00:03:30.480 --> 00:03:34.680
Here we have proved that these evaluations
preserve the norm, with P unchanged.
